<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> <div>The <em>Communist Manifesto</em> opens with the claim that "the history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles." What kind of claim is this?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">A moral claim: that class struggle is unjust and should be eliminated.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">An empirical claim about the fundamental driver of all historical development.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">A prediction about what will happen in the future if capitalism continues.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">A philosophical definition of what a society is.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> <div>In Marx's framework of base and superstructure, which of the following belongs to the <em>base</em>?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">The legal system that enforces property rights.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">The religious institutions that provide social cohesion.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">The relations of production — who owns the means of production and who must sell their labor.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">The political parties that compete for power within the state.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> <div>According to Marx and Engels, what defines a person as a member of the bourgeoisie?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">Having a high income and a comfortable lifestyle.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Owning the means of production and employing the labor of others for profit.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">Being educated and holding professional qualifications.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">Having aristocratic titles or hereditary landholdings.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> <div>Marx and Engels claim that the bourgeoisie produces "its own gravediggers." What do they mean by this?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">Individual capitalists eventually become so wealthy that they can afford to retire and hand power to their heirs.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">Capitalist greed leads to wars that destroy the ruling class from within.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">The development of capitalism necessarily produces and concentrates the proletariat — the very class whose interests require overthrowing capitalism.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">Overproduction leads to economic crises that eventually bankrupt the bourgeoisie.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> <div>Marx and Engels argue that abolishing private property does not mean abolishing <em>personal</em> property. What is the distinction they are drawing?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">Personal property refers to items used for luxury and enjoyment; private property refers to essential goods like food and clothing.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">Personal property is what individuals use in daily life; private property (in their sense) is ownership of the means of production used to employ others and extract profit.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">Private property is protected by law; personal property exists only through social custom.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">Personal property belongs to families; private property belongs to individuals acting alone.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> <div>The ten-point program in Part 2 of the <em>Manifesto</em> lists measures such as progressive income tax, free public education, and nationalization of credit. How does Marx present these measures?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">As a permanent and universal blueprint for a communist society applicable to all countries equally.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">As steps for the transitional period following the workers' seizure of political power, applicable to the most advanced countries.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">As reforms that can be achieved peacefully through democratic voting within capitalism.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">As ideals that can never be fully realized but serve as a moral standard for evaluating existing policies.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> <div>In Part 4 of the <em>Manifesto</em>, how do Marx and Engels describe the relationship between the communists and other workers' and democratic movements?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">Communists regard other movements as rivals to be absorbed or replaced once communism is strong enough.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">Communists support every revolutionary movement against the existing social and political order, regardless of national boundaries.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">Communists remain neutral toward other movements until those movements have proven their commitment to the proletariat's interests.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">Communists oppose democratic movements because democracy within capitalism only reinforces bourgeois power.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> <div>Marx argues that the proletariat has a <em>universal</em> interest — not merely a class interest competing against another class interest. What does he mean?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Workers in every country share the same cultural values, which makes their interests naturally universal.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">The proletariat's interest in ending class exploitation is an interest in abolishing class itself — which benefits all humanity, not just one class.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">Workers outnumber capitalists, so a majority interest is by definition a universal one.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">Because workers produce all the wealth in society, their interests are identical with the interests of society as a whole in the present moment.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> <div>Marx and Engels criticize <em>reactionary socialism</em> despite acknowledging that it sometimes identifies real harms of capitalism. What is their central objection?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">It relies on appeals to emotion rather than rational argument.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">It criticizes capitalism from the perspective of a social order that was itself exploitative, and looks backward toward a world that should not be restored.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">It has no practical political program and is purely academic.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">It only addresses the problems of the petty bourgeoisie and ignores the proletariat entirely.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> <div>What is Marx's objection to <em>bourgeois socialism</em> (also called conservative socialism)?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">It is too radical and threatens to destabilize society before workers are ready to govern themselves.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">It wants the advantages of capitalism for the bourgeoisie while softening its worst effects, without challenging the ownership structure that causes those effects.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">It focuses only on political reform and ignores economic questions altogether.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">It is dishonest — its proponents secretly want to restore feudal society.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q11b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">11</span> <div>Marx respects the <em>critical-utopian socialists</em> (such as Owen, Saint-Simon, and Fourier) more than the other socialist types, but still rejects their approach. Why?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11a" id="q11a">
        <label for="q11a">Their visions of an ideal society are too vague to be useful as political programs.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11b" id="q11b">
        <label for="q11b">They appeal to universal reason and justice to persuade the ruling class, but this strategy cannot work if class interests shape what counts as reasonable and just.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11c" id="q11c">
        <label for="q11c">They focus too narrowly on the conditions of factory workers and ignore agricultural laborers.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11d" id="q11d">
        <label for="q11d">Their writings are too difficult for ordinary workers to understand and therefore cannot build a mass movement.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q12c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">12</span> <div>Marx's concept of alienated labor describes a condition in which workers experience their own labor as external and coerced. Which of the following best captures why Marx considers this a <em>structural</em> problem rather than a problem of individual bad management?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12a" id="q12a">
        <label for="q12a">Most factory owners in the nineteenth century were personally cruel and could have been replaced by more enlightened managers.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12b" id="q12b">
        <label for="q12b">Workers are simply not psychologically suited to finding meaning in repetitive tasks, regardless of economic arrangements.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12c" id="q12c">
        <label for="q12c">Alienation is inherent to wage labor itself — workers must sell their labor as a commodity and do not own the product of their work, regardless of how pleasant the workplace is.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12d" id="q12d">
        <label for="q12d">Workers are alienated because they lack the education needed to understand and appreciate the value of what they produce.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q13c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">13</span> <div>False consciousness, as a concept in Marxist theory, describes which of the following?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13a" id="q13a">
        <label for="q13a">The deliberate deception of workers by capitalists through propaganda and misinformation campaigns.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13b" id="q13b">
        <label for="q13b">The tendency of workers to make irrational economic decisions because they lack education in basic economics.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13c" id="q13c">
        <label for="q13c">A structural condition in which people sincerely hold beliefs about their interests and the justice of social arrangements that systematically serve the interests of the class that exploits them.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13d" id="q13d">
        <label for="q13d">The psychological guilt experienced by bourgeois reformers who recognize the injustice of capitalism but continue to benefit from it.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q14c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">14</span> <div>Marx described his relationship to Hegel as finding Hegel "standing on his head" and setting him right-side up. What does this image mean philosophically?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14a" id="q14a">
        <label for="q14a">Marx replaced Hegel's pessimistic view of history with an optimistic one.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14b" id="q14b">
        <label for="q14b">Marx rejected Hegel's dialectical method and replaced it with straightforward empirical observation.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14c" id="q14c">
        <label for="q14c">Where Hegel derived material reality from Consciousness and Spirit, Marx insisted that consciousness is derived from material conditions of production — reversing the direction of causation.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14d" id="q14d">
        <label for="q14d">Marx applied Hegel's philosophy of history to the future rather than the past, predicting rather than explaining historical development.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q15b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">15</span> <div>One philosophical challenge to Marx's theory of ideology is sometimes called the "self-exemption problem." Which of the following best describes it?</div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15a" id="q15a">
        <label for="q15a">Marx exempted himself from paying taxes on the grounds that capitalist law did not apply to those who opposed it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15b" id="q15b">
        <label for="q15b">If all ideas are shaped by class interests, then Marx's own theory is presumably shaped by his class position — raising the question of how he escapes the ideological distortion he diagnoses in everyone else.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15c" id="q15c">
        <label for="q15c">Marx claimed that communist thinkers were exempt from the class struggle because they represented the interests of all humanity.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15d" id="q15d">
        <label for="q15d">The proletariat is exempt from the need to justify its revolution morally, because historical necessity makes justification unnecessary.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;"></div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;"></div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  

</div>
